Z||000000||grinthock||10-31-2001||11:42 PM||||1FST2M6 you are posting some serious times with some minor mods, what's making the most difference?  Looking at your mods list.<P>Does the APEXI S-AFC unit make a big difference?  What exactally does it do for you?<P>Is the WET kit better?  I am looking at kits, should I go wet?<P><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||11-01-2001||09:05 AM||||SOGGY wet... <A HREF="http://www.nitrouswarehouse.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nitrouswarehouse.com</A>  20216 is the part number... and Engineered Performance messaging the ign, fuel map, intake and exh.. you don't have to spend huge $$ to make decent numbers... but you CAN'T be scared to loose it all... we were SURE my car would have blown up before the first bottle made it through.. i've blasted through 12 bottles now and it's no different than it was before i started spraying.. well actually i have 0 oil pressure... but thats cause the autometer sending unit crapped out and the case split... larger injectors, high fuel pressure.  an S-afc just allows you to make larger injectors support a smaller displacement engine.. you can trim/add a lot or a little.. these same units on a bone stock car will drum up better numbers even with stock injectors! it's all in the tuning... find a decent Dynojet shop... with data logging equipment and book a couple hours... it's $160 an hour plus the welding of a wide band O2 bung. i can drop $400 (like my wifes New Beetle) a month on a car payment or the same on my car thats already paid for... i opted to support a speed habit not the dealership finance department. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||11-01-2001||07:14 PM||||SO you like wet because why?  Why should I go wet instead of dry?  I am willing to spend the extra time/money.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||11-01-2001||08:26 PM||||Because you can run a big ass shot of NOS and not worry about the lean conditions that may occur with a dry kit.<P>But still get a Autometer Air Fuel meter , or a haltech AF-30 from <BR> <A HREF="http://www.twminduction.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twminduction.com</A> <P>A very useful tool. I think a lot of people here are getting the idea that in 4 hours they will be running 12's like 1FST2M6..<P>You just make it look so easy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>There are lots of sites on the net for people considering NOS. The forum is full of topics about NOS kits.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000004||87FieroGTx||11-02-2001||02:13 AM|||| I was running 14.8 then I read 1FST2M6 post 2 weeks later I'm doing 13.5 even with my fooked up 3rd gear <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 Fiero GT ~ auto 3.2 200hp 220tq ~ 75+ shot NOS ~ 0-60 4.9 13.5 1/4||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||11-02-2001||08:29 AM||||grr.. double post... <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||11-02-2001||08:30 AM||||there is <B>NO</B> time difference in installation between wet or dry. unless you go with a 6 fogger set up... my single fogger works just fine why spend the $800 on a 6 fogger system... well just cause you can or if your engine is really built and you want a 250hp shot. my NX kithas just as many connections as the NOS dry i had but instead of tying into the vacuum lines it screws into the fuel rail. it's taken me almost 3 years to get in the 12s! took 4 hours to break 13.6... and 2years and 4 months to shave off another 5 tenths! it's not easy...  and for damn sure not cheap. I've got big $ in recipts from my Se trying different things and failing.. breaking and what not... i just took the trial and error numbers from the Se and adapted them to the GT and added MORE spray and real fuel. on my mods page are some of the changes with those SAME changes anyone can get nearly any 6 in the low 13s... high 12s with decent traction. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||11-02-2001||10:32 AM||||You mention on your page you decreased the overall area of the intake by shaving off  the upper .125.<P>What do you think of increaseing the runner length? Ihave anothermanifold out in the garage I was thinking of cutting to make spacer to raise the manifold off. I see a lot of L98 guys get longer runners to increase low end TQ , I put a spacer on my caddy and got a bit more low down too (carb)<P>What do ya think? worth the effort?<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||11-02-2001||10:47 AM||||that would be great for an auto-X car! or a daily driver even.. i shaved mine to keep the velocitys up... so the nitrous/fuel won't seperate. adding .75" would do wonders for the off idle responce! mine kinda stumbles n/a.   if you could make a plastic pelenum plate i'd buy a set or aluminum even...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> you can drill and tap them for a 6 fogger system. humm... wonder if i could tap cooters tube for a better fogger.. nahh.. 2 is easier.. humm...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stickpoke.gif">  <P>you said .125.. no it's 1 1/4" theres not much left to attach the pelenum to! Shaun said what to ya think i handed it to th emachinist and got it back way shorter! it's on the garage floor now...  i've got 2 middles and 2 tops and am working on a new set up from a fellow HP nut. <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shaun41178||11-02-2001||12:36 PM||||Jon, Increasing runner length will increase low end torque.  Decreasing runner length will give more top end power.  That was the idea behind what travis did.  We both believed that the Fiero intake was more setup for low end and the power dropped off in the high rpm cause of the long runners.  I went out and checked out an intake and there was a little bit of material that could be shaved off and still provide enough thickness to still be able to bolt it up without it cracking.  So I drove down to Atlanta(going that way anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) and I gave Travis an extra upper manifold and he had a buddy shave the top and middle plenum down as much as he could.  I believe Travis dynoed it around a 6hp increase up top but lost some low end which was expected.  But to make up for the loss of low end just add nitrous baby!!  Fixes every torque problem.<P>There is still some more testing that needs to be done with the shaving idea but preliminay tests show an increase in top end power.  Right now it looks like Cooter's double TB intake might be the best way to go for the most top end power<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
